var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Dosearch.jl","page":"Home","text":"Dosearch.jl identifies causal effects from multiple, possibly incomplete data sources. Given input distributions (observational or experimental), a causal graph and a target distribution (the query), it searches for a way to express the query in terms of the inputs with the rules of do-calculus and probability theory. If the search succeeds, it returns the identifying formula.\n\nIt is a Julia port of the R package dosearch (Tikka, Hyttinen and Karvanen, 2021), and gives the same formulas as the R package. Beyond the port, it\n\nwrites the derivation of a formula step by step in LaTeX or Typst, ready for a supplement or appendix (Step-by-step derivations);\nsearches for the maximal amount of latent confounding that can be added to a graph while the query stays identifiable (Maximal identifiable graphs);\ntakes graphs and distributions as Julia values, for problems built in code (Building problems in code).","title":"Dosearch.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Installation","page":"Home","text":"The package is not registered yet:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/eteppo/Dosearch.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#A-first-example","page":"Home","text":"The causal effect of x on y in a graph where z confounds them is identified by adjusting for z:\n\njulia> using Dosearch\n\njulia> r = dosearch(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", \"\"\"\n           x -> y\n           z -> x\n           z -> y\n       \"\"\");\n\njulia> r.identifiable\ntrue\n\njulia> r.formula\n\"\\\\sum_{z}\\\\left(p(z)p(y|x,z)\\\\right)\"\n\njulia> r.derivation\np(y|do(x))\n  = Σ_z p(y,z|do(x))             (marginalization)\n  = Σ_z p(z|do(x)) p(y|do(x),z)  (chain rule)\n  = Σ_z p(z) p(y|do(x),z)        (rule 3: (z ⊥⊥ x) in G_{x̅})\n  = Σ_z p(z) p(y|x,z)            (rule 2: (y ⊥⊥ x | z) in G_{x̲})","title":"A first example"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"guide.md\", \"tutorial.md\", \"ldag.md\", \"derivations.md\", \"maximal.md\", \"values.md\", \"differences.md\", \"api.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#References","page":"Home","text":"Tikka, S., Hyttinen, A. and Karvanen, J. (2021). Causal effect identification from multiple incomplete data sources: a general search-based approach. Journal of Statistical Software 99(5). doi:10.18637/jss.v099.i05\nTikka, S., Hyttinen, A. and Karvanen, J. (2019). Identifying causal effects via context-specific independence relations. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 32.\nPearl, J. (1995). Causal diagrams for empirical research. Biometrika 82(4), 669–688.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api.html#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"using Dosearch brings the names of normal usage: dosearch, DosearchProblem, latex, typst, maximal_identifiable_graphs, maximal_identifiable_graph, BidirectedPair, LatentCause, confounding_candidates and with_candidates. The other names below are public but not exported: write Dosearch.CausalGraph, or import them with using Dosearch: CausalGraph.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api.html#Identification","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Identification"},{"category":"section","location":"api.html#Graphs-and-distributions-as-values","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Graphs and distributions as values"},{"category":"section","location":"api.html#Derivations","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Derivations"},{"category":"section","location":"api.html#Rules-and-search-strategies","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Rules and search strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api.html#Maximal-identifiable-graphs","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Maximal identifiable graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"api.html#Internals","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api.html#Dosearch.Dosearch","page":"API reference","text":"Dosearch\n\nIdentification of causal effects from multiple, possibly incomplete, observational and experimental data sources by a search over do-calculus and probability manipulations. A Julia port of the R package dosearch (Tikka, Hyttinen and Karvanen, 2021, Journal of Statistical Software 99(5), doi:10.18637/jss.v099.i05).\n\nThe main entry point is dosearch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.Dosearch"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.dosearch","page":"API reference","text":"dosearch(data, query, graph; kwargs...) -> DosearchResult\ndosearch(problem::DosearchProblem; kwargs...) -> DosearchResult\n\nIdentify the causal query from the input distributions data in the causal model graph, by a search over do-calculus and probability manipulations (Tikka, Hyttinen and Karvanen, 2021). The graph is either a DAG with latent confounders or a labeled DAG (LDAG) with context-specific independences (Tikka, Hyttinen and Karvanen, 2019). This is a port of dosearch of the R package of the same name, and gives the same formulas.\n\nSyntax\n\ndata lists the input distributions, one per line, as a string or a vector of strings. A distribution is written p(A|do(B),C): A, B and C are comma separated variable names, and both the conditioning variables C and the do-operator are optional (p(A|C,do(B)) is also allowed). For example\n\ndata = \"\"\"\n    p(z|do(x))\n    p(w,y|do(z,x))\n    p(w,y,x|z)\n\"\"\"\n\nA distribution can also be given as a NamedTuple of variable roles: 0 for the left-hand side, 1 for intervened and 2 for conditioning variables, so (y = 0, x = 1) is p(y|do(x)).\n\nquery is the target distribution in the same syntax.\n\ngraph has one edge per line: x -> y for a directed edge and x <-> y (or x -- y) for a bidirected edge, that is, an unobserved common cause of x and y. The parts of an edge are separated by whitespace.\n\nLDAGs\n\nA graph with edge labels is an LDAG. A label is a comma separated list of value assignments of other parents of the child, and the edge vanishes in the context that matches it. An edge that vanishes in several contexts is listed once for each (or, as in the R package, with the labels separated by ;):\n\ngraph = \"\"\"\n    X -> Y : A = 1\n    Z -> Y : A = 0\n    Z -> Y : B = 1, C = 0\n\"\"\"\n\nLDAGs have no bidirected edges: latent variables are explicit nodes that do not appear in the data. Interventions are explicit intervention nodes whose names start with I_, such as I_X -> X, and distributions condition on them instead of using the do-operator: p(Y|X,I_X=1). In general, variables in distributions of an LDAG can be assigned 0 or 1.\n\nValues\n\nFor problems built in code, the inputs can be Julia values instead of text: data a P or a vector of them (mixed with text if needed), query a P, and graph a CausalGraph.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nModel:\n\ntransportability: comma separated transportability nodes, which mark the discrepancies between domains, or a collection of their names.\nselection_bias: comma separated selection bias nodes, or a collection of their names.\nmissing_data: comma separated missing data mechanisms r_x : x, where r_x is the response indicator of x, or pairs \"r_x\" => \"x\". The proxy variable x* of x is added automatically. A response indicator can be fixed to the observed value in distributions as r_x = 1.\nmd_sym = \"1\": the value that marks an enabled response indicator.\ncontext_vars: names of further context variables of an LDAG, besides those in the edge labels and those fixed to a value in the query or an input.\nwarn = true: warn about inputs that are valid but probably unintended.\n\nTransportability, selection bias and missing data are only supported for DAGs.\n\nSearch:\n\nheuristic = false: expand the most promising terms first instead of in the order they were found.\nstrategy: the SearchStrategy that orders the search, ScoreHeuristic() if heuristic = true and FoundOrder() otherwise.\nimprove = true: leave out redundant rules, and stop early when the query is trivially not identifiable.\nrules: the rules to use, in order, as R rule codes or as Rules for DAGs and CsiRules for LDAGs. Defaults to default_rules and default_csi_rules.\ntime_limit = Inf: stop the search after this many seconds (checked before each term is expanded; 0 stops at once). R gives the limit in hours.\nterm_limit: stop the search once it has derived this many terms (input distributions included; see the field terms of the result), checked before each term is expanded. Unlike time_limit, the result does not depend on the machine.\ncache = true: for LDAGs, reuse context-specific independences that were found to hold, as the R package does by default.\n\nOutput:\n\nformula = true: compute the identifying formula and its derivation (see latex and typst).\ndraw_derivation = false: return the derivation as a DOT graph, in the field dot.\ndraw_all = false: draw every derivation step, not only the steps that lead to the query.\nbenchmark_rules = false: record the time spent in each rule.\nverbose = false: print the steps of the search, to io = stdout.\n\nDifferences from the R package: times are in seconds, draw_all draws the steps in the order they were found, and up to 32 variables are supported in DAGs (R: 15) and 64 in LDAGs (R: 30). Edges of an LDAG that vanish in every context are left out of the contexts too; R keeps them there by mistake.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r = dosearch(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", \"\"\"\n           x -> y\n           z -> x\n           z -> y\n       \"\"\");\n\njulia> r.formula\n\"\\\\sum_{z}\\\\left(p(z)p(y|x,z)\\\\right)\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.dosearch"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.DosearchResult","page":"API reference","text":"DosearchResult\n\nThe result of dosearch.\n\nidentifiable::Bool: whether the query was identified.\nlimit_reached::Bool: whether the search stopped at time_limit or term_limit before it could decide; the query is then not identified, but may be identifiable.\nformula::Union{Nothing,String}: the identifying formula in LaTeX, in the notation of the R package, or nothing if the query was not identified or formula = false.\nderivation::Union{Nothing,Derivation}: the Derivation of the formula, which latex and typst write out step by step (nothing if there is no formula).\ndot::Union{Nothing,String}: the derivation as a DOT graph if draw_derivation = true (derivation in the R package).\ntime::Float64: seconds spent searching.\nterms::Int: the number of terms the search derived, input distributions included (what term_limit limits).\nrule_times::Vector{Float64}: seconds spent in each rule of rules if benchmark_rules = true.\nrules: the rules that the search used, in order.\nproblem::DosearchProblem: the problem that was solved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.DosearchResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.isidentifiable","page":"API reference","text":"isidentifiable(result::DosearchResult) -> Bool\n\nWhether the query of result was identified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.isidentifiable"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.DosearchProblem","page":"API reference","text":"DosearchProblem\n\nAbstract type of validated identification problems: DagProblem and LdagProblem. DosearchProblem(data, query, graph; kwargs...) builds an LdagProblem if the graph has edge labels and a DagProblem otherwise. See dosearch for the syntax of the arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.DosearchProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.DagProblem","page":"API reference","text":"DagProblem(data, query, graph; transportability, selection_bias,\n           missing_data, md_sym = \"1\", warn = true)\n\nA validated causal effect identification problem in a DAG with latent confounders: identify the query from the input distributions data in the causal model graph. See dosearch for the syntax of the arguments.\n\nThe fields hold the problem in internal form: labels are the variable names by index, and data and query are Dist terms over those indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.DagProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.LdagProblem","page":"API reference","text":"LdagProblem(data, query, graph; context_vars = String[], warn = true)\n\nA validated identification problem in a labeled DAG: identify the query from the input distributions data in the LDAG graph. See dosearch for the syntax. context_vars names extra variables to treat as context variables, besides those that appear in edge labels and those fixed to a value in the query or an input.\n\nIn internal form, a term Dist over this problem has the left-hand side a, the right-hand side b, and the variables assigned to 0 in c and to 1 in d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.LdagProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.CausalGraph","page":"API reference","text":"CausalGraph(edges...)\n\nA causal graph as a value: the same graph as the text syntax of dosearch, for building and combining graphs in code. The edges are\n\nfrom => to, a directed edge, with names as strings or symbols;\nBidirectedPair(x, y), an unobserved common cause of x and y;\nLabeledEdge(from => to, assignments...), an edge of an LDAG that vanishes in a context;\na graph in the text syntax, or another CausalGraph, whose edges are included in order;\na collection or generator of edges.\n\nRepeated edges are kept once. The order of the edges is kept: it fixes the numbering of the variables, and so the order in which formulas list them. Two graphs are equal if they have the same edges in the same order. string(g) is the graph in the text syntax, and g ∪ h contains the edges of both. Wherever a problem takes a graph, a CausalGraph can be given.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Dosearch: CausalGraph\n\njulia> g = CausalGraph(\"x\" => \"y\", \"z\" => \"x\", \"z\" => \"y\")\nCausalGraph with 3 edges:\n  x -> y\n  z -> x\n  z -> y\n\njulia> g ∪ CausalGraph(BidirectedPair(\"x\", \"y\"))\nCausalGraph with 4 edges:\n  x -> y\n  z -> x\n  z -> y\n  x <-> y\n\njulia> chain = CausalGraph(\"A$k\" => \"A$(k + 1)\" for k in 1:3);\n\njulia> print(chain)\nA1 -> A2\nA2 -> A3\nA3 -> A4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.CausalGraph"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.LabeledEdge","page":"API reference","text":"LabeledEdge(from => to, assignments...)\n\nAn edge of an LDAG that vanishes in a context: the assignments, pairs variable => value with the values 0 or 1, of other parents of to. LabeledEdge(\"X\" => \"Y\", \"A\" => 1) is the line X -> Y : A = 1 of a graph. An edge with several labeled edges vanishes in each of their contexts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.LabeledEdge"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.P","page":"API reference","text":"P(variables...; given = (), intervened = ())\n\nA distribution as a value: P(\"y\"; given = \"z\", intervened = \"x\") is p(y|do(x),z) in the text syntax of dosearch, for building inputs and queries in code. given and intervened take a name or a collection of names, as strings or symbols. A variable can be assigned a value by a pair name => value: 0 or 1 in an LDAG, as in P(\"Y\"; given = [\"X\", \"I_X\" => 1]), and the symbol of an enabled missing data mechanism (md_sym, 1 by default) in a DAG, as in P(\"x*\"; given = \"r_x\" => 1).\n\nstring(d) is the distribution in the text syntax. Wherever a problem takes its input distributions or its query, a P or a vector of them can be given.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Dosearch: CausalGraph, P\n\njulia> P(\"y\"; given = \"z\", intervened = \"x\")\np(y | do(x), z)\n\njulia> dosearch([P(\"x\", \"y\", \"z\")], P(\"y\"; intervened = \"x\"), CausalGraph(\"x\" => \"y\", \"z\" => \"x\", \"z\" => \"y\"))\n\\sum_{z}\\left(p(z)p(y|x,z)\\right)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.P"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.Derivation","page":"API reference","text":"Derivation\n\nHow the search derived an identified query. Stored in the derivation field of a DosearchResult.\n\nWritten out, the derivation starts from the query and rewrites one term at a time by the rule that derived it, until only known terms remain. The last line is then the identifying formula. Each step names its rule. A do-calculus step also gives the d-separation that licenses it, in the mutilated graph of the rule. An LDAG step gives the context-specific independence instead.\n\nlatex(d) and typst(d) write the derivation for a document, and d displays as plain text. The keyword inputs = true continues the derivation past the terms that the formula keeps. Those terms are computed from an input distribution by probability manipulations alone, and the rewriting stops only at the input distributions themselves.\n\nThe fields are internal: the problem, the derivation's nodes of the search (node 1 is the query), the rewrite of each derived node and the identifying formula in the notation of the R package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.Derivation"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Markdown.latex","page":"API reference","text":"latex(d::Derivation; inputs = false, labels = Dict()) -> String\nlatex(r::DosearchResult; kwargs...) -> String\n\nThe derivation d, or the derivation of the identified query of r, as a LaTeX align* environment (package amsmath). This adds methods to Markdown.latex. The first line is the query. Each following line rewrites one term and names the rule it uses in a second column; the last line is the identifying formula. Long derivations may need \\allowdisplaybreaks to break across pages.\n\nWith inputs = true, terms computed from an input distribution by probability manipulations are rewritten too, down to the input distributions. labels maps variable names to LaTeX code that replaces the default: one letter as is, longer names in \\mathit, the part after the first _ as a subscript and a trailing * as a superscript.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r = dosearch(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", \"\"\"\n           x -> y\n           z -> x\n           z -> y\n       \"\"\");\n\njulia> print(latex(r))\n\\begin{align*}\np(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x)) &= \\sum_{z} p(y, z \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x)) && \\text{marginalization} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x)) p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x), z) && \\text{chain rule} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z) p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x), z) && \\text{rule 3: } (z \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp x)_{\\mathcal{G}_{\\overline{x}}} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z) p(y \\mid x, z) && \\text{rule 2: } (y \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp x \\mid z)_{\\mathcal{G}_{\\underline{x}}}\n\\end{align*}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Markdown.latex"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.typst","page":"API reference","text":"typst(d::Derivation; inputs = false, labels = Dict()) -> String\ntypst(r::DosearchResult; kwargs...) -> String\n\nThe derivation d, or the derivation of the identified query of r, as a Typst block equation. The first line is the query. Each following line rewrites one term and names the rule it uses in a second column; the last line is the identifying formula. Long derivations may need #show math.equation: set block(breakable: true) to break across pages.\n\nWith inputs = true, terms computed from an input distribution by probability manipulations are rewritten too, down to the input distributions. labels maps variable names to Typst code that replaces the default: one letter as is, longer names in italic, the part after the first _ as a subscript and a trailing * as a superscript.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.typst"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.Rule","page":"API reference","text":"Rule\n\nThe inference rules of the search. The integer value of each rule is the rule code of the R package, so Rule(-2) is ActionToObservation, and a rule list can be given either as Rules or as R codes.\n\nRule Code Derivation (current term ⇒ new term)\nInsertObservation 1 P(yx)  P(yxz) by do-calculus rule 1\nDeleteObservation -1 P(yxz)  P(yx) by do-calculus rule 1\nObservationToAction 2 P(yxz)  P(yxdo(z)) by do-calculus rule 2\nActionToObservation -2 P(yxdo(z))  P(yxz) by do-calculus rule 2\nInsertAction 3 P(yx)  P(yxdo(z)) by do-calculus rule 3\nDeleteAction -3 P(yxdo(z))  P(yx) by do-calculus rule 3\nMarginalize 4 P(yzx)  P(yx)\nCondition 5 P(yzx)  P(yxz)\nProductConditional 6 P(yxz) and P(zx)  P(yzx)\nProductMarginal -6 P(yx) and P(zxy)  P(yzx)\nDivideMarginal 7 P(yx)  P(zx)  P(yzxz)\nDivideConditional -7 P(yx)  P(zxyz)  P(yzx)\nDenominatorMarginal 8 P(yzx)  P(yx)  P(zxy)\nDenominatorConditional -8 P(yzx)  P(yxz)  P(zx)\nEnableMechanism 9 enable (fix) a missing data mechanism\nExchangeProxy 10 replace a proxy variable with its true variable\n\nInputDistribution (code 0) marks the input distributions in a derivation and is not a rule. Rules 7 to 10 only apply to missing data problems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.Rule"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.default_rules","page":"API reference","text":"default_rules(; missing_data::Bool, improve::Bool) -> Vector{Rule}\n\nThe rules the search tries, in order, unless a rule list is given. With improve = true the redundant observation insertion and deletion rules are left out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.default_rules"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.CsiRule","page":"API reference","text":"CsiRule\n\nThe inference rules of the search for LDAGs. The integer value of each rule is its rule code in the R package (which are different from the codes of Rule), so CsiRule(5) is CsiCaseByCaseZero.\n\nRule Code Derivation (current term ⇒ new term)\nCsiMarginalize 0 P(yzx)  P(yx)\nCsiCondition 1 P(yzx)  P(yxz)\nCsiProductConditional 2 P(yxz) and P(zx)  P(yzx)\nCsiProductMarginal -2 P(yx) and P(zxy)  P(yzx)\nCsiInsertObservation 3 P(yx)  P(yxz) by a CSI\nCsiDeleteObservation -3 P(yxz)  P(yx) by a CSI\nCsiInsertZero 4 P(yx)  P(yxz=0) by a CSI\nCsiInsertOne -4 P(yx)  P(yxz=1) by a CSI\nCsiCaseByCaseZero 5 P(yz=0x) and P(yz=1x)  P(yzx)\nCsiCaseByCaseOne -5 P(yz=1x) and P(yz=0x)  P(yzx)\nCsiGeneralByCase 6 P(yx) and P(yz=vx)  P(yz=1-vx), from the term with z assigned\nCsiGeneralByCaseZero 7 P(yx) and P(yz=0x)  P(yz=1x)\nCsiGeneralByCaseOne -7 P(yx) and P(yz=1x)  P(yz=0x)\nCsiCaseByGeneralZero 8 P(yzx)  P(yz=0x)\nCsiCaseByGeneralOne -8 P(yzx)  P(yz=1x)\n\nCsiInputDistribution marks the input distributions and is not a rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.CsiRule"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.default_csi_rules","page":"API reference","text":"default_csi_rules() -> Vector{CsiRule}\n\nThe rules the search for LDAGs tries, in order, unless a rule list is given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.default_csi_rules"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.SearchStrategy","page":"API reference","text":"SearchStrategy\n\nAbstract type of the strategies that choose which derived term the search expands next. See FoundOrder and ScoreHeuristic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.SearchStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.FoundOrder","page":"API reference","text":"FoundOrder()\n\nExpand terms in the order they were derived (heuristic = FALSE in R).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.FoundOrder"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.ScoreHeuristic","page":"API reference","text":"ScoreHeuristic()\n\nExpand the term that looks closest to the query first, by the score of Tikka et al. (2021) (heuristic = TRUE in R).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.ScoreHeuristic"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.maximal_identifiable_graphs","page":"API reference","text":"maximal_identifiable_graphs(problem; candidates, formulas = false, heuristic,\n                            improve, rules, time_limit, term_limit) -> MaximalGraphs\nmaximal_identifiable_graphs(data, query, graph; kwargs...)\n\nAll maximal sets of candidates that can be added to the model of problem while the query stays identifiable by dosearch, and all minimal sets that make it non-identifiable. The result is a vector of the maximal sets; see MaximalGraphs for its other fields. Build the problem with a set added with with_candidates.\n\ncandidates defaults to confounding_candidates: every latent confounder between a pair of variables that can be added. Candidates of other kinds, such as LatentCause, can be given instead. If the query is not identifiable in the original model there are no maximal sets, and the empty set is the only minimal non-identifiable set. With formulas = true, the identifying formula of each maximal graph is computed too. heuristic, improve and rules are passed to dosearch. The first maximal set is the one that maximal_identifiable_graph finds.\n\nFor an LDAG, a candidate adds a latent node with edges into its variables, which may be labeled (see LatentCause). The variables in the labels of the candidates are context variables in every search, including the one of the original model, so that larger sets of candidates cannot gain case distinctions; problem in the result is the problem with them.\n\ntime_limit (seconds) and term_limit (derived terms) limit each search, as in dosearch. A set whose search stops at a limit counts as non-identifiable and is reported as undecided. Every reported maximal set is still identifiable, but it may not be maximal. With term_limit the result is reproducible; with time_limit it depends on the machine, its load and the number of threads. The formulas are computed without the limits.\n\nLimits pay off when some searches cost far more than the others. A limit that also stops searches that would identify the query makes the result coarse, since every superset of an undecided set counts as non-identifiable too. Adding confounding shrinks the space of derivable terms, so the search in the original model is a guide: set the limit above its terms or time (from dosearch(problem)).\n\nThe sets are found by dualize and advance (Gunopulos et al., 2003), which checks each maximal set and each minimal non-identifiable set of candidates once, plus the checks needed to extend sets to maximal ones. It relies on adding candidates never making a query identifiable, which holds for latent confounders: they only remove d-separations. A set is known to be identifiable without a search when all d-separations used by an earlier derivation of the query still hold, so most searches are of non-identifiable sets. Searches run on several threads when Julia has them (e.g. julia --threads=auto); the result does not depend on the number of threads.\n\nExamples\n\nIn the chain x -> z -> y, the effect of x on y stays identifiable if x and y are confounded (by the front-door formula) or if z and y are confounded, but not both:\n\njulia> graph = \"\"\"\n           x -> z\n           z -> y\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> maximal_identifiable_graphs(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", graph)\n2 maximal identifiable sets of 3 candidates:\n  y <-> z\n  x <-> y\n2 minimal non-identifiable sets:\n  x <-> z\n  y <-> z; x <-> y\n\nConfounding x and z alone, or y and z together with x and y, makes the effect non-identifiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.maximal_identifiable_graphs"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.maximal_identifiable_graph","page":"API reference","text":"maximal_identifiable_graph(problem; candidates, heuristic, improve, rules,\n                           time_limit, term_limit) -> Union{Nothing,Vector{<:Candidate}}\nmaximal_identifiable_graph(data, query, graph; kwargs...)\n\nOne maximal set of candidates that can be added to the model of problem while the query stays identifiable, found greedily: each candidate is added in turn if the query stays identifiable. Earlier candidates are preferred, so the order of candidates decides which maximal set is found. Needs at most one search per candidate. Returns nothing if the query is not identified in the original model. See maximal_identifiable_graphs for the arguments; with a limit, a candidate whose search stops at it is left out, so the set may not be maximal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.maximal_identifiable_graph"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.MaximalGraphs","page":"API reference","text":"MaximalGraphs{C}\n\nThe result of maximal_identifiable_graphs: the maximal identifiable sets of candidates of type C. It is a vector of these sets, and also has the fields\n\nmaximal::Vector{Vector{C}}: the maximal sets of candidates that can be added while the query stays identifiable;\nminimal_nonidentifiable::Vector{Vector{C}}: the minimal sets of candidates that make the query non-identifiable. Every set that contains none of them (and none of the undecided sets) is identifiable, and no other set is;\nundecided::Vector{Vector{C}}: the minimal sets whose search stopped at time_limit or term_limit. They count as non-identifiable, so if there are any, the maximal sets are identifiable but may not be maximal, and some minimal non-identifiable sets may be missing;\nformulas::Vector{String}: the identifying formula with each maximal set added, if formulas = true was given, and empty otherwise;\ncandidates::Vector{C} and problem::DosearchProblem: the inputs;\nsearches::Int: the number of searches that were needed.\n\nEach set lists its candidates in the order of candidates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.MaximalGraphs"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.Candidate","page":"API reference","text":"Candidate\n\nAbstract type of the additions to a causal model that maximal_identifiable_graphs chooses from, such as BidirectedPair.\n\nAdding candidates to a model must only add paths to the graph, so that a query that is identifiable with a set of candidates stays identifiable with any subset of it. To add a kind of candidate, define Dosearch.confounded_pairs(problem::DagProblem, candidate), which returns the bidirected edges that the candidate adds as pairs of variable indices, and Dosearch.latent_edges(problem::LdagProblem, candidate), which returns the edges of the latent node that the candidate adds to an LDAG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.Candidate"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.BidirectedPair","page":"API reference","text":"BidirectedPair(x, y)\n\nThe candidate that adds the bidirected edge x <-> y, i.e. a latent common cause of the variables named x and y. The pair is unordered. In an LDAG, which has no bidirected edges, it adds a latent node U_x_y with the edges U_x_y -> x and U_x_y -> y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.BidirectedPair"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.LatentCause","page":"API reference","text":"LatentCause(variables...; labels = [])\nLatentCause(variables; labels = [])\n\nThe candidate that adds a latent common cause of all the named variables: a bidirected edge between every pair of them, added together. LatentCause(\"x\", \"y\") adds the same edge as BidirectedPair(\"x\", \"y\"). The order of the variables does not matter.\n\nIn an LDAG, it adds a latent node with an edge into each of the variables. labels gives some of these edges a label, as pairs child => label in the syntax of the graph: LatentCause(\"x\", \"y\"; labels = [\"y\" => \"a = 1\"]) is a latent cause of x and y whose edge into y vanishes where a = 1. The variables of a label must be parents of the child. The search for maximal graphs treats them as context variables throughout (see maximal_identifiable_graphs). An edge into a variable with an intervention node I_x also vanishes where I_x = 1, like the other edges into an intervened variable. Labels are not supported for DAGs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.LatentCause"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.confounding_candidates","page":"API reference","text":"confounding_candidates(problem) -> Vector{BidirectedPair}\n\nEvery bidirected edge that can be added to the model of problem: all pairs of variables that are not already bidirected, except pairs with a transportability node or a missing data proxy. The pairs are ordered by the internal variable numbering.\n\n\n\n\n\nconfounding_candidates(problem::LdagProblem) -> Vector{BidirectedPair}\n\nEvery pair of variables of the LDAG of problem that can get a latent common cause, i.e. all pairs without intervention nodes, ordered by the internal variable numbering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.confounding_candidates"},{"category":"function","location":"api.html#Dosearch.with_candidates","page":"API reference","text":"with_candidates(problem, candidates) -> DosearchProblem\n\nThe problem whose causal model has the candidates added.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.with_candidates"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.VarSet","page":"API reference","text":"VarSet\n\nAn immutable set of variable indices in 1:64, stored as a bitmask.\n\nVarSet supports the usual set operations (∪, ∩, setdiff, ⊆, in, isempty, length) and iterates over its elements in increasing order. Construct one from a collection of indices, as in VarSet([1, 3]) or VarSet(1:3), or from a raw bitmask with VarSet(mask::UInt64).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.VarSet"},{"category":"type","location":"api.html#Dosearch.Dist","page":"API reference","text":"Dist(a, b, c, d)\n\nA distribution term P(A mid do(C) B setminus C) over variable indices.\n\na: the variables on the left-hand side.\nb: every variable on the right-hand side, both conditioning and intervened variables, so c ⊆ b.\nc: the intervened variables, inside do(⋅).\nd: the missing data mechanisms (response indicators) that are enabled, i.e. fixed to the observed value, in this term.\n\nDist is an immutable value type and can be used as a Dict key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Dosearch.Dist"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Identifying-causal-effects","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"dosearch takes three inputs, written as text in the syntax of the R package: the input distributions (data), the target distribution (query) and the causal graph (graph). Problems built in code can give them as Julia values instead; see Building problems in code.","title":"Identifying causal effects"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Distributions","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"A distribution is written p(A|do(B),C), where A, B and C are comma separated variable names. The conditioning variables C and the do-operator are optional, and p(A|C,do(B)) means the same. data lists one distribution per line, as a string or a vector of strings:\n\ndata = \"\"\"\n    p(x,y,z)\n    p(y|do(x),z)\n\"\"\"\n\nA distribution can also be given as a NamedTuple of variable roles: 0 for the left-hand side, 1 for intervened and 2 for conditioning variables, so (y = 0, x = 1) is p(y|do(x)).","title":"Distributions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Graphs","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"The graph has one edge per line: x -> y for a directed edge and x <-> y (or x -- y) for a bidirected edge, i.e. a latent common cause of x and y. The three parts of an edge are separated by whitespace. A graph whose edges have labels is a labeled DAG (LDAG); see LDAGs.\n\nInputs with several lines are easiest to write as multi-line strings, as in the examples here. The package reports an error with a hint for malformed input, such as x->y without spaces or several edges on one line.\n\nThe front-door model, where a latent variable confounds x and y, but the effect of x on y goes through z:\n\njulia> r = dosearch(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", \"\"\"\n           x -> z\n           z -> y\n           x <-> y\n       \"\"\")\n\\sum_{z}\\left(p(z|x)\\sum_{x}\\left(p(x)p(y|z,x)\\right)\\right)\n\njulia> dosearch(\"p(x,y)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", \"\"\"\n           x -> y\n           x <-> y\n       \"\"\")\nThe query p(y|do(x)) is not identifiable by do-search.","title":"Graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Results","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"dosearch returns a DosearchResult. Its fields are:\n\nidentifiable (also isidentifiable): whether the query was identified;\nformula: the identifying formula in LaTeX, in the notation of the R package;\nderivation: the Derivation of the formula, which latex and typst write out step by step (see Step-by-step derivations);\nlimit_reached: whether the search stopped at time_limit or term_limit before it could decide;\ntime and terms: the seconds spent searching and the number of terms the search derived;\ndot: the derivation as a DOT graph, with draw_derivation = true;\nrule_times, rules and problem.\n\nA query is not identifiable by do-search if the search derives every term it can without reaching the query. Do-search is complete for the problems that do-calculus is known to solve completely, such as the identification of causal effects from a single observational distribution.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Several-data-sources","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"The inputs can mix observational and experimental distributions, also over different sets of variables. Here an experiment on z and an observational distribution of x and z identify the effect of x on y:\n\njulia> data = \"\"\"\n           p(x,z)\n           p(y|do(z))\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> graph = \"\"\"\n           x -> z\n           z -> y\n           x <-> y\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> dosearch(data, \"p(y|do(x))\", graph)\n\\sum_{z}\\left(p(z|x)p(y|do(z))\\right)","title":"Several data sources"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Transportability,-selection-bias-and-missing-data","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"Special nodes mark discrepancies between data sources:\n\ntransportability: comma separated transportability nodes, which mark the differences between a source domain and the target domain. A distribution that conditions on a transportability node comes from the source domain.\nselection_bias: comma separated selection bias nodes. A distribution that conditions on a selection node is observed under selection.\nmissing_data: comma separated mechanisms r_x : x, where r_x is the response indicator of x. The proxy variable x* of x (x where observed, missing otherwise) is added automatically. r_x = 1 in a distribution means that x is observed.\n\nA source domain where s changes the distribution of z, with an experiment on x there:\n\njulia> data = \"\"\"\n           p(x,y,z)\n           p(y,z|do(x),s)\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> graph = \"\"\"\n           x -> z\n           z -> y\n           s -> z\n           x <-> y\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> dosearch(data, \"p(y|do(x))\", graph; transportability = \"s\")\n\\sum_{z}\\left(p(z|x)p(y|do(x),z,s)\\right)\n\nMissing values of x and y, whose response indicators depend on y, with the marginal distribution of y known from another source:\n\njulia> data = \"\"\"\n           p(x*,y*,r_x,r_y)\n           p(y)\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> graph = \"\"\"\n           x -> y\n           y -> r_y\n           r_y -> r_x\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> dosearch(data, \"p(y|do(x))\", graph; missing_data = \"r_x : x, r_y : y\")\n\\frac{\\left(p(y)p(x|r_x = 1,y,r_y = 1)\\right)}{\\sum_{y}\\left(p(y)p(x|r_x = 1,y,r_y = 1)\\right)}","title":"Transportability, selection bias and missing data"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Search-options","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"heuristic = true expands the most promising terms first. It often finds identifiable queries much faster; a non-identifiable query costs the same either way.\nrules restricts the search to some of the rules, given as R rule codes or as Rules (CsiRules for LDAGs).\ntime_limit (seconds) and term_limit (derived terms) stop a search that takes too long. The result then has limit_reached = true, and the query is not identified although it may be identifiable. term_limit gives the same result on any machine.\nimprove = false searches with all rules, as the original algorithm does.","title":"Search options"},{"category":"section","location":"guide.html#Problems","page":"Identifying causal effects","text":"DosearchProblem parses and validates the inputs once, and dosearch(problem; kwargs...) searches. It returns a DagProblem or an LdagProblem, depending on the graph.\n\njulia> p = DosearchProblem(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", \"\"\"\n           x -> y\n           z -> x\n           z -> y\n       \"\"\")\nDagProblem with 3 variables\n  query: p(y|do(x))\n  data:  p(y,x,z)\n\njulia> dosearch(p; heuristic = true).identifiable\ntrue","title":"Problems"},{"category":"section","location":"derivations.html#Step-by-step-derivations","page":"Step-by-step derivations","text":"The identifying formula alone does not show why it is right. The derivation field of a result holds how the search derived it, and latex and typst write it out for a document. The derivation starts from the query. Each line rewrites one term and names the rule it uses. The last line is the identifying formula.\n\nFor the front-door model, latex(r) gives\n\njulia> r = dosearch(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", \"\"\"\n           x -> z\n           z -> y\n           x <-> y\n       \"\"\");\n\njulia> print(latex(r))\n\\begin{align*}\np(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x)) &= \\sum_{z} p(z, y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x)) && \\text{marginalization} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x)) p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x), z) && \\text{chain rule} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid x) p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(x), z) && \\text{rule 2: } (z \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp x)_{\\mathcal{G}_{\\underline{x}}} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid x) p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(z, x)) && \\text{rule 2: } (y \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp z \\mid x)_{\\mathcal{G}_{\\overline{x}\\,\\underline{z}}} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid x) p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(z)) && \\text{rule 3: } (y \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp x \\mid z)_{\\mathcal{G}_{\\overline{z}\\,\\overline{x}}} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid x) \\sum_{x'} p(y, x' \\mid \\mathrm{do}(z)) && \\text{marginalization} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid x) \\sum_{x'} p(x' \\mid \\mathrm{do}(z)) p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(z), x') && \\text{chain rule} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid x) \\sum_{x'} p(x') p(y \\mid \\mathrm{do}(z), x') && \\text{rule 3: } (x \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp z)_{\\mathcal{G}_{\\overline{z}}} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{z} p(z \\mid x) \\sum_{x'} p(x') p(y \\mid z, x') && \\text{rule 2: } (y \\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp z \\mid x)_{\\mathcal{G}_{\\underline{z}}}\n\\end{align*}\n\nwhich renders as\n\nbeginaligned\np(y mid mathrmdo(x)) = sum_z p(z y mid mathrmdo(x))  textmarginalization \n= sum_z p(z mid mathrmdo(x)) p(y mid mathrmdo(x) z)  textchain rule \n= sum_z p(z mid x) p(y mid mathrmdo(x) z)  textrule 2  (z perpperp x)_mathcalG_underlinex \n= sum_z p(z mid x) p(y mid mathrmdo(z x))  textrule 2  (y perpperp z mid x)_mathcalG_overlinexunderlinez \n= sum_z p(z mid x) p(y mid mathrmdo(z))  textrule 3  (y perpperp x mid z)_mathcalG_overlinezoverlinex \n= sum_z p(z mid x) sum_x p(y x mid mathrmdo(z))  textmarginalization \n= sum_z p(z mid x) sum_x p(x mid mathrmdo(z)) p(y mid mathrmdo(z) x)  textchain rule \n= sum_z p(z mid x) sum_x p(x) p(y mid mathrmdo(z) x)  textrule 3  (x perpperp z)_mathcalG_overlinez \n= sum_z p(z mid x) sum_x p(x) p(y mid z x)  textrule 2  (y perpperp z mid x)_mathcalG_underlinez\nendaligned\n\n\n\n\nand typst(r) gives the same as a Typst equation:\n\njulia> print(typst(r))\n$ p(y | \"do\"(x)) &= sum_(z) p(z, y | \"do\"(x)) && quad \"marginalization\" \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | \"do\"(x)) p(y | \"do\"(x), z) && quad \"chain rule\" \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | x) p(y | \"do\"(x), z) && quad \"rule 2:\" (z ⫫ x)_(cal(G)_(underline(x))) \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | x) p(y | \"do\"(z, x)) && quad \"rule 2:\" (y ⫫ z | x)_(cal(G)_(overline(x) underline(z))) \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | x) p(y | \"do\"(z)) && quad \"rule 3:\" (y ⫫ x | z)_(cal(G)_(overline(z) overline(x))) \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | x) sum_(x') p(y, x' | \"do\"(z)) && quad \"marginalization\" \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | x) sum_(x') p(x' | \"do\"(z)) p(y | \"do\"(z), x') && quad \"chain rule\" \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | x) sum_(x') p(x') p(y | \"do\"(z), x') && quad \"rule 3:\" (x ⫫ z)_(cal(G)_(overline(z))) \\\n  &= sum_(z) p(z | x) sum_(x') p(x') p(y | z, x') && quad \"rule 2:\" (y ⫫ z | x)_(cal(G)_(underline(z))) $\n\nIn the REPL, the derivation displays as plain text:\n\njulia> r.derivation\np(y|do(x))\n  = Σ_z p(z,y|do(x))                           (marginalization)\n  = Σ_z p(z|do(x)) p(y|do(x),z)                (chain rule)\n  = Σ_z p(z|x) p(y|do(x),z)                    (rule 2: (z ⊥⊥ x) in G_{x̲})\n  = Σ_z p(z|x) p(y|do(z,x))                    (rule 2: (y ⊥⊥ z | x) in G_{x̅,z̲})\n  = Σ_z p(z|x) p(y|do(z))                      (rule 3: (y ⊥⊥ x | z) in G_{z̅,x̅})\n  = Σ_z p(z|x) Σ_x' p(y,x'|do(z))              (marginalization)\n  = Σ_z p(z|x) Σ_x' p(x'|do(z)) p(y|do(z),x')  (chain rule)\n  = Σ_z p(z|x) Σ_x' p(x') p(y|do(z),x')        (rule 3: (x ⊥⊥ z) in G_{z̅})\n  = Σ_z p(z|x) Σ_x' p(x') p(y|z,x')            (rule 2: (y ⊥⊥ z | x) in G_{z̲})","title":"Step-by-step derivations"},{"category":"section","location":"derivations.html#Notation","page":"Step-by-step derivations","text":"Do-calculus steps give the d-separation that licenses them in Pearl's form: (Y perpperp Z mid X W)_mathcalG_overlineXunderlineZ is the d-separation in the graph without the edges into X and out of Z. For rule 3, the second overlined set is Pearl's Z(W): the nodes of Z that are not ancestors of W.\nLDAG steps give the context-specific independence instead, such as (Y perpperp X mid A = 1).\nSteps of probability calculus are named: marginalization, conditioning, chain rule, cases, law of total probability and evaluation (of a function of A at A = 0, written f_A = 0). Missing data steps replace a proxy by its true variable where it is observed. A do-calculus step that intervenes on a response indicator sets it to 1, as in the R package, and the derivation writes out that the term it rewrites to is evaluated there, such as p(y^* mid r_y = 1 x). The formula of the R package leaves this implicit: p(y^* mid r_y x).\nA summation variable that would repeat a name in use is primed, as the inner x above, where the formula of the R package sums over x again.","title":"Notation"},{"category":"section","location":"derivations.html#Options","page":"Step-by-step derivations","text":"inputs = true: the formula keeps terms that are computed from one input distribution by probability manipulations alone, such as p(z mid x) above. With inputs = true the derivation rewrites those too, down to the input distributions.\nlabels: a dictionary from variable names to code that replaces their default rendering. By default, names longer than one letter are set in italics, the part after the first _ becomes a subscript (rendered the same way, so R_Y₁ is R_Y_1), trailing subscript digits such as ₁ become a subscript and a trailing * (proxy variables) a superscript.\n\njulia> r = dosearch(\"p(x,y)\", \"p(y|x)\", \"x -> y\");\n\njulia> print(typst(r; inputs = true, labels = Dict(\"x\" => \"X\", \"y\" => \"Y\")))\n$ p(Y | X) &= frac(p(Y, X), sum_(Y') p(Y', X)) && quad \"conditioning\" $","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"derivations.html#Documents","page":"Step-by-step derivations","text":"The LaTeX output is an align* environment of the amsmath package. Long derivations need \\allowdisplaybreaks in the preamble to break across pages. The Typst output is a block equation; long derivations need #show math.equation: set block(breakable: true).","title":"Documents"},{"category":"section","location":"values.html#Building-problems-in-code","page":"Building problems in code","text":"The text syntax is the main way to write a problem: it reads like the mathematics and matches the R package. When a program builds the problem instead, for example a graph over many time points or parts combined in several ways, text assembled by interpolation and concatenation is easy to get wrong. The inputs can then be Julia values:\n\na CausalGraph of edges: \"x\" => \"y\", BidirectedPair(\"x\", \"y\") and, in an LDAG, LabeledEdge(\"x\" => \"y\", \"z\" => 1);\na distribution P: P(\"y\"; given = \"z\", intervened = \"x\") is p(y|do(x),z).\n\nThey are not exported, so a program imports them: using Dosearch: CausalGraph, LabeledEdge, P. The values render to the text syntax and go through the same parser, so they give the same problems as the text. Their constructors check names, self-loops and cycles where the values are built, so that a name cannot change the meaning of the text. Every function that takes a graph, input distributions or a query takes them, and the input distributions can mix values and text.","title":"Building problems in code"},{"category":"section","location":"values.html#Generated-graphs","page":"Building problems in code","text":"A treatment at each visit, with a covariate that the earlier treatment affects and that shares an unmeasured cause with the outcome:\n\njulia> using Dosearch: CausalGraph, LabeledEdge, P\n\njulia> visits = 0:1;\n\njulia> g = CausalGraph((\"L$k\" => \"A$k\" for k in visits), (\"A$k\" => \"Y\" for k in visits),\n                       \"L0\" => \"Y\", \"A0\" => \"L1\", \"L0\" => \"L1\", BidirectedPair(\"L1\", \"Y\"))\nCausalGraph with 8 edges:\n  L0 -> A0\n  L1 -> A1\n  A0 -> Y\n  A1 -> Y\n  L0 -> Y\n  A0 -> L1\n  L0 -> L1\n  L1 <-> Y\n\njulia> data = P([\"L$k\" for k in visits]..., [\"A$k\" for k in visits]..., \"Y\")\np(L0, L1, A0, A1, Y)\n\njulia> query = P(\"Y\"; intervened = [\"A$k\" for k in visits])\np(Y | do(A0, A1))\n\njulia> dosearch(data, query, g)\n\\sum_{L1,L0}\\left(p(Y|A0,A1,L1,L0)\\left(p(L0)p(L1|A0,L0)\\right)\\right)","title":"Generated graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"values.html#Combining-graphs","page":"Building problems in code","text":"Graphs combine with ∪. A graph in the text syntax converts to a CausalGraph, and string gives the text back, so the two forms mix. The order of the edges is kept, because it fixes the order of the variables in formulas.\n\njulia> print(CausalGraph(\"x -> y\") ∪ CausalGraph(\"z\" => \"y\"))\nx -> y\nz -> y","title":"Combining graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"values.html#LDAGs-and-values","page":"Building problems in code","text":"An edge of an LDAG with a label is a LabeledEdge, and a variable of a distribution is assigned a value by a pair:\n\njulia> lg = CausalGraph(\"I_X\" => \"X\", LabeledEdge(\"L\" => \"X\", \"I_X\" => 1), \"L\" => \"Y\", \"X\" => \"Y\");\n\njulia> dosearch(P(\"L\", \"X\", \"Y\"), P(\"Y\"; given = [\"X\", \"I_X\" => 1]), lg)\n\\sum_{L}\\left(p(L)p(Y|X,L)\\right)","title":"LDAGs and values"},{"category":"section","location":"values.html#Special-nodes","page":"Building problems in code","text":"Transportability and selection bias nodes are collections of names, as in selection_bias = [\"s\"], and missing data mechanisms are pairs of a response indicator and its variable:\n\njulia> dosearch([P(\"x*\", \"y*\", \"r_x\", \"r_y\"), P(\"y\")], P(\"y\"; intervened = \"x\"),\n                CausalGraph(\"x\" => \"y\", \"y\" => \"r_y\", \"r_y\" => \"r_x\");\n                missing_data = [\"r_x\" => \"x\", \"r_y\" => \"y\"])\n\\frac{\\left(p(y)p(x|r_x = 1,y,r_y = 1)\\right)}{\\sum_{y}\\left(p(y)p(x|r_x = 1,y,r_y = 1)\\right)}","title":"Special nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"maximal.html#Maximal-identifiable-graphs","page":"Maximal identifiable graphs","text":"How much unmeasured confounding can a causal model contain before the query stops being identifiable? maximal_identifiable_graphs answers this for a set of candidate additions to the graph, such as latent confounders. It finds every maximal set of candidates that can be added while the query stays identifiable, and every minimal set that makes it non-identifiable. Every set that contains none of the minimal sets is identifiable, and no other set is.\n\nIn the chain x -> z -> y, the effect of x on y stays identifiable if x and y are confounded (by the front-door formula) or if z and y are confounded, but not both:\n\njulia> graph = \"\"\"\n           x -> z\n           z -> y\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> r = maximal_identifiable_graphs(\"p(x,y,z)\", \"p(y|do(x))\", graph; formulas = true)\n2 maximal identifiable sets of 3 candidates:\n  y <-> z\n  x <-> y\n2 minimal non-identifiable sets:\n  x <-> z\n  y <-> z; x <-> y\n\njulia> r.formulas[2]\n\"\\\\sum_{z}\\\\left(p(z|x)\\\\sum_{x}\\\\left(p(x)p(y|z,x)\\\\right)\\\\right)\"\n\nThe result is a vector of the maximal sets, with further fields; see MaximalGraphs. maximal_identifiable_graph finds one maximal set greedily, with at most one search per candidate.","title":"Maximal identifiable graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"maximal.html#Candidates","page":"Maximal identifiable graphs","text":"Candidates are Candidates. By default they are confounding_candidates: a BidirectedPair for every pair of variables, a latent common cause of the two. A LatentCause of several variables adds one latent common cause of all of them. with_candidates builds the problem with a set of candidates added.","title":"Candidates"},{"category":"section","location":"maximal.html#How-it-works","page":"Maximal identifiable graphs","text":"Adding confounding only adds paths to the graph, so it only removes independences, and every derivation in the larger model is valid in the smaller one. The identifiable sets of candidates are therefore closed under taking subsets. The search uses this:\n\nIt finds all maximal sets by dualize and advance (Gunopulos et al., 2003): each maximal and each minimal non-identifiable set is searched once, plus the searches that extend sets to maximal ones.\nIt answers from earlier searches whenever monotonicity decides the answer.\nA set is identifiable without a search if every independence that an earlier derivation of the query used still holds.\nSearches run on all threads (julia --threads=auto). The result does not depend on the number of threads.\n\nMost of the time goes to the searches that end non-identifiable, which have to derive every term they can.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"maximal.html#Limits","page":"Maximal identifiable graphs","text":"time_limit (seconds) and term_limit (derived terms) stop each search that takes too long. A set whose search stops counts as non-identifiable and is listed in undecided. The reported maximal sets are then still identifiable but may not be maximal. With term_limit the result is the same on any machine.\n\nA limit pays off when some searches cost far more than the others. A limit that also stops searches that would identify the query makes the result coarse, since every superset of an undecided set counts as non-identifiable too. The search of the original model is a guide: adding confounding shrinks the space of derivable terms, so set the limit above the terms (or time) of dosearch(problem).","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"maximal.html#LDAGs","page":"Maximal identifiable graphs","text":"In an LDAG, which has no bidirected edges, a candidate adds a latent node with edges into its variables. The edges can be labeled, which makes the confounding context-specific. Edges into an intervened variable vanish under the intervention, as the other edges into it do.\n\njulia> p = DosearchProblem(\"p(A,X,Y)\", \"p(Y|X,I_X=1)\", \"\"\"\n           I_X -> X\n           X -> Y : A = 1\n           A -> Y\n           A -> X : I_X = 1\n       \"\"\");\n\njulia> specific = LatentCause(\"X\", \"Y\"; labels = [\"X\" => \"A = 0\"]);\n\njulia> maximal_identifiable_graphs(p; candidates = [specific, BidirectedPair(\"X\", \"Y\")])\n1 maximal identifiable set of 2 candidates:\n  latent cause of X, Y (into X : A = 0)\n1 minimal non-identifiable set:\n  X <-> Y\n\nConfounding of X and Y keeps the effect identifiable if it vanishes where A = 0, since X has no effect on Y where A = 1. The variables in the labels of candidates are context variables in every search, so that larger sets of candidates cannot gain case distinctions.","title":"LDAGs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#Tutorial:-Bayesian-g-formula-with-Turing.jl","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"This tutorial combines Dosearch.jl with Turing.jl in a Bayesian causal inference workflow:\n\nWrite a model of a follow-up study in DynamicPPL, with unmeasured confounding, and simulate data from it.\nIdentify a causal effect nonparametrically with Dosearch.jl, with loss to follow-up encoded in three ways, and find how much unmeasured confounding the identification tolerates.\nFit the model to the data, predict the outcomes under each intervention with the posterior, and compute the effect from the predictions.\n\nusing Dosearch, Turing, Enzyme, Random, Statistics","title":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula with Turing.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#A-follow-up-study","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"Patients visit a clinic at times k = 0 1 ldots K - 1. At visit k, we measure their severity L_k and decide on their treatment A_k. Before the next visit, a patient may be lost to follow-up (C_k+1 = 1), and otherwise we learn whether they died (Y_k+1 = 1). Nothing is observed after loss to follow-up or death, and death is absorbing.\n\nSeverity confounds treatment at every visit, and severity is itself affected by earlier treatment. Two causes are not measured: the patient's access to care V affects their severity at baseline and every treatment decision, and their frailty U affects their later severity and death. Loss to follow-up depends only on the measured past.\n\nEvery variable has a logistic model in its past, with coefficients shared over time, and the unmeasured causes are standard normal. The loadings lambda geq 0 fix the direction of U and V. The same model generates the data and is fitted to them. It creates its variables itself, and data enter it only by conditioning. The argument intervention is a rule that sets treatment from the history of severity, and it prevents loss to follow-up. Without it, treatment and loss to follow-up follow their models.\n\n@model function followup(N, K; intervention = nothing)\n    # column k + 1 of L and A is visit k, column k of C and Y the interval\n    # that ends at visit k\n    L, A, C, Y = (Matrix{Bool}(undef, N, K) for _ in 1:4)\n    α ~ Normal(0, 1.5)                                  # severity L₀\n    θL ~ filldist(Normal(0, 1.5), 3)                    # severity Lₖ: 1, Lₖ₋₁, Aₖ₋₁\n    θA ~ filldist(Normal(0, 1.5), 3)                    # treatment Aₖ: 1, Lₖ, Aₖ₋₁\n    θC ~ filldist(Normal(0, 1.5), 3)                    # loss Cₖ₊₁: 1, Lₖ, Aₖ\n    θY ~ filldist(Normal(0, 1.5), 4)                    # death Yₖ₊₁: 1, L₀, Lₖ, Aₖ\n    λ ~ filldist(truncated(Normal(0, 1); lower = 0), 4) # V → L₀, V → A, U → L, U → Y\n    U ~ filldist(Normal(), N)                           # frailty\n    V ~ filldist(Normal(), N)                           # access to care\n    for i in 1:N\n        for k in 1:K                                    # visit k - 1 and the interval after it\n            if k == 1\n                L[i, k] ~ BernoulliLogit(α + λ[1] * V[i])\n            else\n                L[i, k] ~ BernoulliLogit(θL[1] + θL[2] * L[i, k - 1] + θL[3] * A[i, k - 1] + λ[3] * U[i])\n            end\n            if intervention === nothing\n                previous = k == 1 ? false : A[i, k - 1]\n                A[i, k] ~ BernoulliLogit(θA[1] + θA[2] * L[i, k] + θA[3] * previous + λ[2] * V[i])\n                C[i, k] ~ BernoulliLogit(θC[1] + θC[2] * L[i, k] + θC[3] * A[i, k])\n            else\n                A[i, k] = intervention(L[i, 1:k])\n                C[i, k] = false\n            end\n            C[i, k] && break                            # lost: nothing more is observed\n            Y[i, k] ~ BernoulliLogit(θY[1] + θY[2] * L[i, 1] + θY[3] * L[i, k] + θY[4] * A[i, k] + λ[4] * U[i])\n            Y[i, k] && break                            # dead\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nTo simulate a study, we set the parameters to their true values with fix and draw from the model. We keep what is observed: L, A, C and Y, with missing for what was not measured.\n\ntrue_parameters = (α = -0.4, θL = [-1.0, 1.5, -1.0], θA = [-0.5, 1.0, 1.0],\n                   θC = [-2.5, 0.5, 0.3], θY = [-2.0, 0.8, 1.0, -1.0], λ = [0.8, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0])\n\nvalue(draw, vn) = haskey(draw, vn) ? draw[vn] : missing\nobservations(draw, N, K) = (L = [value(draw, @varname(L[i, k])) for i in 1:N, k in 1:K],\n                            A = [value(draw, @varname(A[i, k])) for i in 1:N, k in 1:K],\n                            C = [value(draw, @varname(C[i, k])) for i in 1:N, k in 1:K],\n                            Y = [value(draw, @varname(Y[i, k])) for i in 1:N, k in 1:K])\n\nN, K = 1000, 2\ndata = observations(rand(Xoshiro(1), fix(followup(N, K), true_parameters)), N, K)\n(lost = count(isequal(true), data.C), died = count(isequal(true), data.Y))\n(lost = 192, died = 296)","title":"A follow-up study"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#Identification","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"We want the risk of death by the end of follow-up if every patient were treated at every visit, compared with nobody being treated, and nobody were lost to follow-up: P(Y_2 = 1 mid mathrmdo(A_0 = a A_1 = a C_1 = 0 C_2 = 0)) for a = 1 and a = 0. As in Robins' approach to censoring, loss to follow-up is intervened on like a treatment.\n\nWithout knowing the true model, we can still defend its causal graph: which variables may affect which. Here it has an edge for every variable in the model of another one, and the unmeasured causes U and V. Since only survivors are measured, death also points to everything measured after it. Loss to follow-up C_k depends only on the measured past, and it points to nothing: it hides the later variables but does not change them.\n\n(Image: The causal graph of the follow-up study with two intervals)\n\nWe give Dosearch.jl the measured part of the graph, and add the unmeasured causes as candidates, LatentCauses of the variables they affect. The data are what we know: the baseline and the first loss to follow-up for every patient, and then each interval among the patients followed in it.\n\ncausal_graph = \"\"\"\n    L₀ -> A₀\n    L₀ -> Y₁\n    A₀ -> Y₁\n    L₀ -> L₁\n    A₀ -> L₁\n    Y₁ -> L₁\n    A₀ -> A₁\n    L₁ -> A₁\n    Y₁ -> A₁\n    L₀ -> Y₂\n    L₁ -> Y₂\n    A₁ -> Y₂\n    Y₁ -> Y₂\n\"\"\"\ncensoring = \"\"\"\n    L₀ -> C₁\n    A₀ -> C₁\n    Y₁ -> C₂\n    L₁ -> C₂\n    A₁ -> C₂\n\"\"\"\ninputs = \"\"\"\n    p(L₀, A₀, C₁)\n    p(Y₁, L₁, A₁, C₂ | L₀, A₀, C₁)\n    p(Y₂ | L₀, A₀, C₁, Y₁, L₁, A₁, C₂)\n\"\"\"\nproblem = DosearchProblem(inputs, \"p(Y₂ | do(A₀, A₁, C₁, C₂))\", causal_graph * censoring)\nunmeasured = [LatentCause(\"L₀\", \"A₀\", \"A₁\"), LatentCause(\"L₁\", \"Y₁\", \"Y₂\")]\nr = dosearch(with_candidates(problem, unmeasured))\n\\sum_{Y₁,L₁,L₀}\\left(p(L₀)\\left(p(Y₁,L₁|A₀,C₁,L₀)p(Y₂|A₀,Y₁,L₁,A₁,C₁,C₂,L₀)\\right)\\right)\n\nThe effect is identifiable despite U and V, by the g-formula\n\nbeginaligned\nP(y_2 mid mathrmdo(a_0 a_1 C_1 = 0 C_2 = 0)) \nquad = sum_l_0 y_1 l_1 P(l_0) P(y_1 l_1 mid l_0 a_0 C_1 = 0)\nP(y_2 mid l_0 a_0 C_1 = 0 y_1 l_1 a_1 C_2 = 0)\nendaligned\n\nThe appendix derives it step by step.\n\nThe inputs claim more than we know. Conditioning on C_1 means that Dosearch.jl takes P(Y_1 L_1 A_1 C_2 mid L_0 A_0 C_1) as known for both values of C_1, but we know it only for the patients still followed, with C_1 = 0. An input of a DAG cannot fix a variable to a value, so this is the closest we can write. The formula is still valid, because it uses the inputs only where we know them: it conditions on C_1 and C_2 at their intervened values, 0, and never sums over them. A formula that summed over C_1 would use the outcomes of patients after they were lost, so formulas from this encoding need that check. The other encodings below state exactly what we know, and give the same formula.\n\nEach factor is estimated from the patients followed at that time: P(l_0) from all patients, the second factor from those followed in the first interval, and the last from those followed in both. A complete-case analysis, which estimates every factor from the patients followed to the end, would be biased, since loss to follow-up depends on severity and treatment.\n\nNothing is measured after death. For a patient who died in the first interval, we set L_1, A_1 and C_2 to 0, and Y_2 = 1: Y_k is death by visit k. Then no patient who died has A_1 = 1, and the last factor is not estimable for them: positivity fails. It is 1, since death is absorbing, but Dosearch.jl does not know this: it is a fact about the outcome, not about the graph. With the hazards of death h_1 = P(Y_1 = 1 mid l_0 a_0 C_1 = 0) and h_2 = P(Y_2 = 1 mid l_0 a_0 C_1 = 0 Y_1 = 0 l_1 a_1 C_2 = 0), which are estimable, the risk is\n\nbeginaligned\nP(Y_2 = 1 mid mathrmdo(a_0 a_1 C_1 = 0 C_2 = 0)) \nquad = sum_l_0 P(l_0)\nBig(h_1 + (1 - h_1) sum_l_1 P(l_1 mid l_0 a_0 C_1 = 0 Y_1 = 0) h_2Big)\nendaligned","title":"Identification"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#Sequential-exchangeability","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"Two steps of the derivation use rule 2, one per visit: (Y_1 L_1 Y_2 perpperp A_0 C_1 mid L_0 A_1 C_2) in the graph without the edges out of A_0 and C_1 and into A_1 and C_2, and (Y_2 perpperp A_1 C_2 mid L_0 A_0 C_1 Y_1 L_1) in the graph without the edges out of A_1 and C_2. These are sequential exchangeability for treatment and censoring, in graphical form: at each visit, treatment and staying in follow-up are independent of the future given the measured past, with the later treatment and censoring set by the intervention.\n\nSo one search for the joint intervention checks every visit. A search per visit would ask about one treatment at a time, with the others left as they are, and that is a different question. Here access to care V affects both treatments, so the effect of A_0 alone is not identifiable, although the joint effect is:\n\nfirst_alone = DosearchProblem(inputs, \"p(Y₂ | do(A₀, C₁, C₂))\", causal_graph * censoring)\ndosearch(with_candidates(first_alone, unmeasured))\nThe query p(Y₂ | do(A₀, C₁, C₂)) is not identifiable by do-search.","title":"Sequential exchangeability"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#Other-encodings","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"Pearl's school describes the same data with selection nodes or with missingness indicators instead of interventions on loss to follow-up. Dosearch.jl supports both, and they give the same formula. So does an LDAG, which can fix variables to values in the inputs.\n\nA selection node S_k = 1 - C_k is 1 for the patients followed in interval k. A distribution that conditions on a selection node is known only among the selected, which is what we know. The query no longer intervenes on loss to follow-up:\n\nselection_inputs = \"\"\"\n    p(L₀, A₀)\n    p(Y₁, L₁, A₁ | L₀, A₀, S₁)\n    p(Y₂ | L₀, A₀, S₁, Y₁, L₁, A₁, S₂)\n\"\"\"\nselection_graph = causal_graph * \"\"\"\n    L₀ -> S₁\n    A₀ -> S₁\n    Y₁ -> S₂\n    L₁ -> S₂\n    A₁ -> S₂\n\"\"\"\nselection = DosearchProblem(selection_inputs, \"p(Y₂ | do(A₀, A₁))\", selection_graph;\n                            selection_bias = \"S₁, S₂\")\ndosearch(with_candidates(selection, unmeasured))\n\\sum_{Y₁,L₁,L₀}\\left(p(L₀)\\left(p(Y₁,L₁|A₀,L₀,S₁)p(Y₂|A₀,Y₁,L₁,A₁,L₀,S₁,S₂)\\right)\\right)\n\nWith missingness indicators, each variable that may be missing has a response indicator, 1 if the variable is observed, and a proxy, which is the variable where it is observed and missing otherwise: R_Y_1 and Y_1^* for Y_1, and so on. The data are the distribution of the proxies and the indicators, which is what we observe. The indicators of Y_1, L_1 and A_1 are equal, which a graph cannot express, so the graph lets the last two depend on the first. This search takes about a minute.\n\nmissingness_graph = causal_graph * \"\"\"\n    L₀ -> R_Y₁\n    A₀ -> R_Y₁\n    R_Y₁ -> R_L₁\n    R_Y₁ -> R_A₁\n    R_Y₁ -> R_Y₂\n    Y₁ -> R_Y₂\n    L₁ -> R_Y₂\n    A₁ -> R_Y₂\n\"\"\"\nmissingness = DosearchProblem(\"p(L₀, A₀, Y₁*, L₁*, A₁*, Y₂*, R_Y₁, R_L₁, R_A₁, R_Y₂)\",\n                              \"p(Y₂ | do(A₀, A₁))\", missingness_graph;\n                              missing_data = \"R_Y₁ : Y₁, R_L₁ : L₁, R_A₁ : A₁, R_Y₂ : Y₂\")\nr_missingness = dosearch(with_candidates(missingness, unmeasured))\n\\sum_{Y₁,L₁,L₀}\\left(\\left(p(L₀)p(Y₁*,L₁*|R_Y₁,R_L₁,A₀,L₀)\\right)p(Y₂|Y₁,R_Y₁ = 1,L₁,R_L₁ = 1,A₁,R_A₁ = 1,R_Y₂ = 1,A₀,L₀)\\right)\n\nBoth are the g-formula above, with selection or observation (S = 1 or R = 1) in place of the intervention C = 0. Writing R = 1 for all four response indicators being 1:\n\nbeginaligned\nP(y_2 mid mathrmdo(a_0 a_1)) \nquad = sum_l_0 y_1 l_1 P(l_0) P(y_1 l_1 mid l_0 a_0 S_1 = 1)\nP(y_2 mid l_0 a_0 y_1 l_1 a_1 S_1 = S_2 = 1) \nquad = sum_l_0 y_1 l_1 P(l_0) P(y_1^* l_1^* mid l_0 a_0 R_Y_1 = R_L_1 = 1)\nP(y_2 mid l_0 a_0 y_1 l_1 a_1 R = 1)\nendaligned\n\nThe notation of the R package, which the formula follows, leaves R_Y_1 = R_L_1 = 1 implicit in the second factor; the derivation writes it out. The derivations also show how the encodings meet. Before either uses the data, a step by rule 3 intervenes on the selection nodes or on the response indicators, which is valid because these affect none of the other variables. So the Pearl-style derivations intervene on loss to follow-up too. The appendix shows the derivation with missingness indicators.\n\nFinally, a labeled DAG can say literally that the inputs are known for C_1 = 0 and C_2 = 0, since its distributions can fix variables to values. Interventions are explicit nodes such as I_A_0: the edges into an intervened variable vanish when its intervention node is 1. The unmeasured causes are explicit latent nodes, and the query sets C_1 = C_2 = 0 with their intervention nodes.\n\nldag_graph = \"\"\"\n    I_A₀ -> A₀\n    I_A₁ -> A₁\n    I_C₁ -> C₁\n    I_C₂ -> C₂\n    V -> L₀\n    V -> A₀ : I_A₀ = 1\n    L₀ -> A₀ : I_A₀ = 1\n    L₀ -> C₁ : I_C₁ = 1\n    A₀ -> C₁ : I_C₁ = 1\n    U -> Y₁\n    L₀ -> Y₁\n    A₀ -> Y₁\n    U -> L₁\n    L₀ -> L₁\n    A₀ -> L₁\n    Y₁ -> L₁\n    V -> A₁ : I_A₁ = 1\n    A₀ -> A₁ : I_A₁ = 1\n    L₁ -> A₁ : I_A₁ = 1\n    Y₁ -> A₁ : I_A₁ = 1\n    Y₁ -> C₂ : I_C₂ = 1\n    L₁ -> C₂ : I_C₂ = 1\n    A₁ -> C₂ : I_C₂ = 1\n    U -> Y₂\n    L₀ -> Y₂\n    L₁ -> Y₂\n    A₁ -> Y₂\n    Y₁ -> Y₂\n\"\"\"\nldag_inputs = \"\"\"\n    p(L₀, A₀, C₁)\n    p(Y₁, L₁, A₁, C₂ | L₀, A₀, C₁ = 0)\n    p(Y₂ | L₀, A₀, C₁ = 0, Y₁, L₁, A₁, C₂ = 0)\n\"\"\"\nldag_query = \"p(Y₂ | A₀, A₁, C₁ = 0, C₂ = 0, I_A₀ = 1, I_A₁ = 1, I_C₁ = 1, I_C₂ = 1)\"\ndosearch(DosearchProblem(ldag_inputs, ldag_query, ldag_graph))\n\\sum_{L₀,Y₁,L₁}\\left(p(L₀)\\left(p(Y₁,L₁|A₀,C₁ = 0,L₀)p(Y₂|A₀,A₁,C₁ = 0,C₂ = 0,L₀,Y₁,L₁)\\right)\\right)\n\nThis is the g-formula exactly as written above.","title":"Other encodings"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#Unmeasured-confounding","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"How much unmeasured confounding could there be? The maximal identifiable graphs answer this: every maximal set of pairs of variables that can have an unmeasured common cause while the effect stays identifiable. We search with selection nodes, where Dosearch.jl uses the data only among the patients followed, so every set it finds is identifiable from what we know. We assume that loss to follow-up shares no unmeasured cause with the other variables, so the candidates are the pairs of the other variables.\n\nselected = (\"S₁\", \"S₂\")\ncandidates = [c for c in confounding_candidates(selection) if c.x ∉ selected && c.y ∉ selected]\nmaximal = maximal_identifiable_graphs(selection; candidates)\nlength(maximal)\n13\n\nOur confounding is one of these maximal sets: an unmeasured common cause of any further pair of variables would make the effect non-identifiable.\n\nours = Set(BidirectedPair(x, y) for c in unmeasured for x in c.variables for y in c.variables if x < y)\nours in Set.(maximal)\ntrue","title":"Unmeasured confounding"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#Estimation","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"We condition the model on the data with | and sample from the posterior with NUTS, using Enzyme for the gradients. Conditioning on a matrix with missing values conditions on the observed values only. The likelihood then has a factor for each variable of each patient while followed, so each part of the model is fitted to the patients followed at that time, as in the identifying formula.\n\nmodel = followup(N, K) | data\nchain = sample(Xoshiro(2), model, NUTS(; adtype = AutoEnzyme()), 500; progress = false)\n\nAn intervention is a rule from the history of severity to treatment. Besides the static rules, always and never treat, it can be dynamic, such as treating whenever the current severity is high. The same g-formula identifies such a rule, with a_k set by the rule.\n\nalways(history) = true\nnever(history) = false\nwhen_severe(history) = last(history)\n\nFor each intervention, predict simulates the follow-up of the patients from their observed baseline severity and their posterior frailty, for every posterior draw. This computes the identifying formula by simulation (the parametric g-formula), since the fitted model contains every factor of it. Then we compute the risk of death by the end of follow-up in each draw.\n\nfunction predict_followup(chain, data, intervention)\n    N, K = size(data.Y)\n    baseline = (L = [k == 1 ? data.L[i, 1] : missing for i in 1:N, k in 1:K],)\n    return predict(Xoshiro(3), followup(N, K; intervention) | baseline, chain)\nend\n\nfunction risk(predictions, N, K)\n    deaths = zeros(size(predictions, 1))\n    for i in 1:N\n        died = falses(length(deaths))\n        for k in 1:K\n            died .|= isequal.(vec(predictions[@varname(Y[i, k])]), true)\n        end\n        deaths .+= died\n    end\n    return deaths ./ N\nend\n\nrisks = Dict(rule => risk(predict_followup(chain, data, rule), N, K)\n             for rule in (always, never, when_severe))\n\nThe posterior of the effects, as risk differences with never treating:\n\nsummarize(draws) = (mean = round(mean(draws), digits = 3),\n                    interval_95 = round.(quantile(draws, [0.025, 0.975]), digits = 3))\n(always = summarize(risks[always] .- risks[never]),\n when_severe = summarize(risks[when_severe] .- risks[never]))\n(always = (mean = -0.229, interval_95 = [-0.301, -0.158]), when_severe = (mean = -0.122, interval_95 = [-0.171, -0.077]))\n\nTo check them, we simulate a large population from the true model under each intervention:\n\nfunction true_risk(intervention; N = 100_000, K = 2)\n    draw = rand(Xoshiro(0), fix(followup(N, K; intervention), true_parameters))\n    return mean(any(k -> isequal(value(draw, @varname(Y[i, k])), true), 1:K) for i in 1:N)\nend\n(always = round(true_risk(always) - true_risk(never), digits = 3),\n when_severe = round(true_risk(when_severe) - true_risk(never), digits = 3))\n(always = -0.194, when_severe = -0.102)\n\nTreating at every visit reduces the risk of death substantially, treating only when severe less, and the 95% posterior intervals cover the true effects.\n\nThe workflow carries over to other studies and longer follow-ups. Dosearch.jl tells from the causal graph alone whether an effect is a function of the observed data, and how much unmeasured confounding that survives. A model of the data then gives the posterior of the effect.","title":"Estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial.html#Appendix:-derivation","page":"Tutorial: Bayesian g-formula","text":"The derivation of the identifying formula from the query, by latex:\n\nHTML{String}(\"<p class=\\\"math-container\\\">\\\\[\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&amp;p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{0}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2})) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, Y_{2}, L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{0}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2})) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{marginalization} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{0}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2})) p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{0}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2}), L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{chain rule} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{0}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2}), L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 3: } (L_{0} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{A_{0}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2}\\\\})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\overline{A_{0}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, C_{2}), A_{0}, C_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 2: } (\\\\{Y_{1}, L_{1}, Y_{2}\\\\} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{A_{0}, C_{1}\\\\} \\\\mid A_{1}, C_{2}, L_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\overline{A_{1}, C_{2}}\\\\,\\\\underline{A_{0}, C_{1}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}, L_{1} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, C_{2}), A_{0}, C_{1}, L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, C_{2}), A_{0}, Y_{1}, L_{1}, C_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{chain rule} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}, L_{1} \\\\mid A_{0}, C_{1}, L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, C_{2}), A_{0}, Y_{1}, L_{1}, C_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 3: } (\\\\{Y_{1}, L_{1}\\\\} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{A_{1}, C_{2}\\\\} \\\\mid A_{0}, C_{1}, L_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\overline{A_{1}, C_{2}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}, L_{1} \\\\mid A_{0}, C_{1}, L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid A_{0}, Y_{1}, L_{1}, A_{1}, C_{1}, C_{2}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 2: } (Y_{2} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{A_{1}, C_{2}\\\\} \\\\mid A_{0}, Y_{1}, L_{1}, C_{1}, L_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\underline{A_{1}, C_{2}}}}\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]</p>\")\n\nWith missingness indicators instead, the first steps intervene on the response indicators:\n\nHTML{String}(\"<p class=\\\"math-container\\\">\\\\[\\\\begin{aligned}\\n&amp;p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0})) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, Y_{2}, L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0})) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{marginalization} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0})) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{chain rule} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{0})) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 3: } (\\\\{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}\\\\} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp A_{1} \\\\mid A_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\overline{A_{0}}\\\\,\\\\overline{A_{1}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0})) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 3: } (\\\\{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}\\\\} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}\\\\} \\\\mid A_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\overline{A_{0}}\\\\,\\\\overline{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(Y_{1}^{*}, L_{1}^{*}, L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0})) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad Y_{1}^{*} = Y_{1} \\\\text{ if } R_{Y_{1}} = 1, L_{1}^{*} = L_{1} \\\\text{ if } R_{L_{1}} = 1 \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0})) p(Y_{1}^{*}, L_{1}^{*} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0}), L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{chain rule} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}^{*}, L_{1}^{*} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0}), L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 3: } (L_{0} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, A_{0}\\\\})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\overline{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, A_{0}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}^{*}, L_{1}^{*} \\\\mid R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0}, L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(A_{1}, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 2: } (\\\\{Y_{1}^{*}, L_{1}^{*}\\\\} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, A_{0}\\\\} \\\\mid L_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\underline{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, A_{0}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}^{*}, L_{1}^{*} \\\\mid R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0}, L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid \\\\mathrm{do}(R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{1}, R_{A_{1}} = 1, R_{Y_{2}} = 1, A_{0}), Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 3: } (Y_{2} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, R_{A_{1}}, R_{Y_{2}}\\\\} \\\\mid Y_{1}, L_{1}, A_{1}, A_{0}, L_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\overline{A_{1}, A_{0}}\\\\,\\\\overline{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, R_{A_{1}}, R_{Y_{2}}}}} \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;= \\\\sum_{Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0}} p(L_{0}) p(Y_{1}^{*}, L_{1}^{*} \\\\mid R_{Y_{1}} = 1, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{0}, L_{0}) p(Y_{2} \\\\mid Y_{1}, R_{Y_{1}} = 1, L_{1}, R_{L_{1}} = 1, A_{1}, R_{A_{1}} = 1, R_{Y_{2}} = 1, A_{0}, L_{0}) \\\\\\\\\\n&amp;\\\\qquad \\\\text{rule 2: } (Y_{2} \\\\perp\\\\!\\\\!\\\\!\\\\perp \\\\{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, A_{1}, R_{A_{1}}, R_{Y_{2}}, A_{0}\\\\} \\\\mid Y_{1}, L_{1}, L_{0})_{\\\\mathcal{G}_{\\\\underline{R_{Y_{1}}, R_{L_{1}}, A_{1}, R_{A_{1}}, R_{Y_{2}}, A_{0}}}}\\n\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\\\]</p>\")","title":"Appendix: derivation"},{"category":"section","location":"ldag.html#LDAGs","page":"LDAGs","text":"A labeled DAG (LDAG) encodes context-specific independences: an edge can vanish in some contexts, i.e. for some values of other parents of its child. An edge label is a comma separated list of value assignments, and the edge vanishes in the context that matches it. An edge that vanishes in several contexts is listed once for each:\n\ngraph = \"\"\"\n    X -> Y : A = 1\n    Z -> Y : A = 0\n    Z -> Y : B = 1, C = 0\n\"\"\"\n\nHere X -> Y vanishes where A = 1, and Z -> Y where A = 0 or where B = 1 and C = 0. (The R package writes the alternatives of a label on one line, separated by ;, as in Z -> Y : A = 0; B = 1, C = 0. That works too.) The labeled variables (A, B and C) are context variables, and they are binary. So is every variable fixed to a value in the query or an input distribution. More context variables can be named with context_vars.\n\nLDAGs have no bidirected edges: latent variables are explicit nodes that appear in no distribution. Interventions are explicit intervention nodes whose names start with I_, and the edges into an intervened variable vanish where its intervention node is 1. A distribution conditions on the intervention node instead of using the do-operator: p(Y|X,I_X=1) is the effect of X on Y. In general, variables in distributions of an LDAG can be assigned the values 0 and 1.\n\njulia> graph = \"\"\"\n           I_X -> X\n           X -> Y : A = 1\n           L -> X : I_X = 1\n           L -> X : A = 0\n           L -> Y\n           A -> X : I_X = 1\n           A -> Y\n       \"\"\";\n\njulia> r = dosearch(\"p(A,X,Y)\", \"p(Y|X,I_X=1)\", graph)\n\\sum_{A}\\left[\\left(p(Y|X,A = 0)\\left[p(A)\\right]_{A = 0}\\right) /\\ \\left(p(Y|A = 1)\\left[p(A)\\right]_{A = 1}\\right)\\right]\n\nThe latent L confounds X and Y except where A = 0, and X has no effect on Y where A = 1, so the effect is identified case by case: the formula [f /\\ g] is f where A = 0 and g where A = 1. The step-by-step derivation spells this out:\n\njulia> r.derivation\np(Y|X,I_X = 1)\n  = Σ_A p(Y,A|X,I_X = 1)                                                  (marginalization)\n  = Σ_A {p(Y,A = 0|X,I_X = 1) if A = 0; p(Y,A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}    (cases)\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0,I_X = 1) p(A = 0|X,I_X = 1) if A = 0; p(Y,A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (chain rule)\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) p(A = 0|X,I_X = 1) if A = 0; p(Y,A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (CSI: (Y ⊥⊥ I_X | X,A = 0))\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A|X,I_X = 1)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y,A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (evaluation)\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A|I_X = 1)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y,A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (CSI: (A ⊥⊥ X | I_X = 1))\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y,A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (CSI: (A ⊥⊥ I_X))\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y|X,A = 1,I_X = 1) p(A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (chain rule)\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y|A = 1,I_X = 1) p(A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (CSI: (Y ⊥⊥ X | A = 1,I_X = 1))\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y|A = 1) p(A = 1|X,I_X = 1) if A = 1}  (CSI: (Y ⊥⊥ I_X | A = 1))\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y|A = 1) [p(A|X,I_X = 1)]_{A = 1} if A = 1}  (evaluation)\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y|A = 1) [p(A|I_X = 1)]_{A = 1} if A = 1}  (CSI: (A ⊥⊥ X | I_X = 1))\n  = Σ_A {p(Y|X,A = 0) [p(A)]_{A = 0} if A = 0; p(Y|A = 1) [p(A)]_{A = 1} if A = 1}  (CSI: (A ⊥⊥ I_X))\n\nThe search for LDAGs uses its own rules (CsiRule), which add case distinctions over context variables to the rules of probability, and the independences of a context instead of do-calculus. Transportability, selection bias and missing data are only supported for DAGs.","title":"LDAGs"},{"category":"section","location":"differences.html#Differences-from-the-R-package","page":"Differences from R","text":"Dosearch.jl gives the same formulas as R dosearch 1.0.12, character for character: the test suite compares against the R package on its examples and tests and on several hundred random problems. The rule order, the order in which subsets of variables are tried and the search order all follow the R package. The differences are these.","title":"Differences from the R package"},{"category":"section","location":"differences.html#Interface","page":"Differences from R","text":"Options are keyword arguments instead of a control list.\nTimes are in seconds (time_limit in R is in hours). time_limit = 0 stops at once; no limit is Inf. term_limit is a deterministic alternative, and the result tells whether a search stopped at its limit (limit_reached).\nThe DOT graph of draw_derivation = true is in the field dot. The field derivation holds the step-by-step derivation.\ndraw_all = true draws the derivation steps in the order they were found, not in hash table order.\nUp to 32 variables are supported in DAGs (R: 15) and 64 in LDAGs (R: 30).","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"differences.html#Inputs","page":"Differences from R","text":"Dosearch.jl rejects some inputs that R accepts, with an error that explains the syntax:\n\nan empty graph or no input distributions, where R reports the query as not identifiable;\nthe characters \\n (a backslash and n, as in a raw string), which are not a line break;\nvariable names in the graph with (, ), ,, | or =, which no distribution could refer to;\nunknown keyword arguments and rule codes, and negative limits.\n\nIt warns about variables of the distributions that are not in the graph, which are unconnected and probably a typo (warn = false turns warnings off). Other differences:\n\nThe non-rule code 0 in rules is rejected; R applies it as a copy of the previous rule.\nValue assignments such as x = 1 without missing data mechanisms are rejected.\nVariable names may contain do, as in dose.\nLDAG label values other than 0 and 1 are rejected; R ignores them.","title":"Inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"differences.html#LDAGs","page":"Differences from R","text":"An edge can be listed on several lines, one label on each, which means the same as the labels separated by ; on one line.\nEdges that vanish in every context are left out of the contexts. R means to do this too, but keeps them because of a comparison bug. All the R fixtures give the same results anyway.\nThe CSI criterion looks up the contexts of a statement by the variables that appear in labels. R also uses context variables given with con_vars that appear in no label. It then finds no contexts, and its reasoning by cases accepts false independences, such as that of a variable and its parent, so it can identify a query that is not identifiable. The two agree when the assigned context variables all appear in labels, as in all the R fixtures.\nA variable fixed to a value in the query or an input distribution is a context variable, as if it were in con_vars. R assigns values only to the variables in labels and in con_vars, so without con_vars it cannot identify, for example, p(Y|C=0,I_C=1) in the LDAG I_C -> C, X -> C : I_C = 1, X -> Y, even from p(X,C,Y), although it equals p(Y). With con_vars, R gives the same result as Dosearch.jl.","title":"LDAGs"}]
}
